WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.176
The limits of automated RAG metrics.

2
00:00:02.456 --> 00:00:06.403
RAGAS-style metrics are useful, and they are LLM judges.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:09.264
Everything week 2 taught about judges applies.

4
00:00:09.544 --> 00:00:11.592
Four limits matter most in practice.

5
00:00:11.872 --> 00:00:13.749
They need calibration on your data.

6
00:00:14.029 --> 00:00:26.381
A faithfulness judge has a true positive rate and a true negative rate like any other, and lenient judges are common: they accept claims that sound like the passage but add a condition or a promise.

7
00:00:26.661 --> 00:00:33.275
The lab’s recorded judge catches only half of the unsupported claims, which inflates faithfulness noticeably.

8
00:00:33.555 --> 00:00:39.933
Measure your judge against an expert’s labels before you trust its number, exactly as you did for other judges.

9
00:00:40.213 --> 00:00:53.141
ARES (Saad-Falcon and colleagues, 2023) builds this into the method: it uses a few hundred human-annotated examples to correct its automated judges’ estimates, with confidence intervals.

10
00:00:53.421 --> 00:00:55.491
Claim splitting is a design choice.

11
00:00:55.771 --> 00:00:58.651
How you break an answer into claims changes the score.

12
00:00:58.931 --> 00:01:08.168
Split too coarsely and one unsupported clause hides inside a supported sentence; split too finely and trivial fragments pad the denominator.

13
00:01:08.448 --> 00:01:12.395
Fix the splitter, version it, and don’t compare scores across splitters.

14
00:01:12.675 --> 00:01:14.125
Scores are not portable.

15
00:01:14.405 --> 00:01:15.920
A faithfulness of 0.

16
00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:21.704
87 from one judge model, prompt, and library version is not comparable to 0.

17
00:01:21.984 --> 00:01:23.371
87 from another.

18
00:01:23.651 --> 00:01:29.709
Treat a judge change like a ruler change: rescore the baseline with the new judge before you compare.

19
00:01:29.989 --> 00:01:31.717
Consistency is not truth.

20
00:01:31.997 --> 00:01:38.739
Faithfulness and relevancy can both be high while the answer is wrong, because the context was wrong or stale.

21
00:01:39.019 --> 00:01:56.256
Citations have the same gap: in an audit of generative search engines, Liu, Zhang, and Liang (2023) found only about half of generated sentences were fully supported by their citations, and about a quarter of citations didn’t support their sentence.

22
00:01:56.536 --> 00:02:04.173
Check citation support explicitly if your product shows citations, and keep a reference-answer correctness check on a core set.
